  {:id :s6a-plan-03 :severity :P1
   :title "Plain collection and arbitrary-encoding claims exceed the stated input boundary"
   :evidence "Lines 127-128 and 150-157 require encoded binary masquerading as text and nested illegal shapes to be rejected before any effect, but provide neither an exhaustive admissible text/provenance boundary nor a safe traversal rule. S4A's separate offline review demonstrates persistent collection backing-array accessors can execute on traversal, despite passing outer plain-value checks. Arbitrary strings cannot generally be classified as harmless text versus encoded bytes solely by syntax."
   :settlement "P1 admits only bounded closed, explicitly enumerated plain data, no JS object/record/metadata/proxy/accessor/wrapper or executable values; validate before iteration/coercion via an actually safe representation boundary or refuse untrusted host values before traversal. Test root and nested malicious getters/backing stores with zero invocations. Reject known structured binary/image/URL/data-URI and encoded attachment representations without asserting detection of every possible encoding. Real text requires trusted immutable typed provenance and source bytes at the held runtime boundary; arbitrary caller text cannot establish that fact. If the host cannot safely inspect a proposed value, deny it rather than claiming universal proxy detection."}
  {:id :s6a-plan-04 :severity :P2
   :title "One source reference does not define the promised complete multi-message history"
   :evidence "Proposed input fields lines 150-152 describe one event/content-ref/text, whereas lines 102-105 and 161 require a bounded complete ordered 1..32-message history. There is no specified per-message immutable identity, order, completeness or source/revision binding."
